WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:18.604
As I wrote in my New York Herald article, there was only one good explanation for those mysterious

2
00:00:18.664 --> 00:00:21.926
events at sea: a marine animal of enormous power.

3
00:00:21.946 --> 00:00:27.369
"The great depths of the sea are entirely unknown to us," I wrote in the newspaper.

4
00:00:27.389 --> 00:00:32.832
"Who knows what creatures live twelve or fifteen miles below the surface of the ocean?

5
00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:38.834
There is, however, one known animal powerful enough to pierce the hull of a steamship.

6
00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:44.755
The common narwhal, or sea unicorn, often grows to a length of sixty feet.

7
00:00:45.655 --> 00:00:51.557
Increase its size by five or ten times and you have a gigantic beast capable of causing great

8
00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:56.559
damage." The ordinary narwhal is armed with an ivory tusk as hard as steel.

9
00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:00.940
Some of these tusks have killed whales and penetrated ships' hulls.

10
00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:07.639
One Paris museum possesses a narwhal tusk that is fifteen inches wide at its base and almost seven

11
00:01:07.679 --> 00:01:08.200
feet long.

12
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:16.324
Now suppose that this tusk were six times stronger than usual and the animal ten times more powerful.

13
00:01:17.205 --> 00:01:21.987
Launch this weapon at high speed, and you would have a force capable of producing a catastrophe at

14
00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:28.291
sea. "Until I receive more information," I concluded in my newspaper article, "I believe that the beast is

15
00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:30.272
a narwhal of colossal dimensions.

16
00:01:31.168 --> 00:01:35.831
Unless, of course, it's something above and beyond anything that anyone has ever seen or imagined."

17
00:01:36.851 --> 00:01:39.673
I realized that I was undercutting my argument with this last sentence.

18
00:01:40.553 --> 00:01:45.436
But I wanted to protect my reputation as a distinguished professor and not give the Americans any more

19
00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:46.576
reasons to laugh at me.

20
00:01:47.557 --> 00:01:50.879
After all, I had just admitted that I believed in a sea monster.

21
00:01:51.839 --> 00:01:57.362
My article was very popular, and soon everyone seemed to have an opinion about the gigantic narwhal.

22
00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:00.204
I still thought that it could exist.

23
00:02:01.012 --> 00:02:06.714
If huge dinosaurs had once roamed the earth, why couldn't an equally large narwhal cruise the seas?

24
00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:11.275
Some people saw the creature's existence as a purely scientific problem.

25
00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:17.957
But more practical people, especially in England and the United States, were determined to rid the ocean of

26
00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:18.497
this beast.

27
00:02:19.377 --> 00:02:21.738
The sea must be made safe for travelers.

28
00:02:22.618 --> 00:02:24.499
The United States led the way in this effort.

29
00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:28.800
In New York preparations were made for an expedition to pursue the narwhal.

30
00:02:29.676 --> 00:02:34.742
The warship Abraham Lincoln was armed and ready to depart under the command of Captain Farragut.

31
00:02:35.503 --> 00:02:39.587
But as soon as it was decided to go after the narwhal, the creature disappeared!

32
00:02:40.288 --> 00:02:42.551
For two months no ship encountered it.

33
00:02:43.352 --> 00:02:44.693
No one knew where to look for it.

34
00:02:45.354 --> 00:02:47.656
The Abraham Lincoln remained at the pier in Brooklyn.

35
00:02:49.078 --> 00:02:56.095
Suddenly on June 2, a steamship on its way from California to Shanghai reported that it had seen

36
00:02:56.155 --> 00:03:00.177
the animal three weeks earlier in the northern part of the Pacific Ocean.

37
00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:02.818
Everyone was extremely excited.

38
00:03:03.518 --> 00:03:06.119
Captain Farragut did not want to waste another minute.

39
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.382
The <Abraham Lincoln> was quickly stocked with food and coal, and I soon received the following letter at

40
00:03:12.422 --> 00:03:19.583
my New York hotel: Dear Professor Aronnax: The government of the United States invites you to join the

41
00:03:19.703 --> 00:03:23.066
Abraham Lincoln expedition as a representative from France.

42
00:03:23.986 --> 00:03:27.970
Captain Farragut will provide a cabin for your use during the expedition.

43
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.272
The ship will be departing in three hours.

44
00:03:32.273 --> 00:03:34.114
Very cordially yours, J.B.

45
00:03:34.154 --> 00:03:36.116
Hobson Secretary of the U.S.

46
00:03:36.176 --> 00:03:43.342
Navy Three seconds before this letter arrived, I had no intention of pursuing the gigantic narwhal.

47
00:03:44.262 --> 00:03:50.093
Three seconds after it arrived, I felt my whole purpose in life was to chase down this creature.

48
00:03:51.013 --> 00:03:53.534
I was still tired from my long trip to Nebraska.

49
00:03:54.254 --> 00:03:59.936
I wanted nothing more than to see my country, my friends, and my little apartment near the botanical

50
00:03:59.976 --> 00:04:02.576
gardens. But nothing could hold me back.

51
00:04:03.337 --> 00:04:09.698
I forgot everything—fatigue, friends, and home—and accepted without hesitation the offer from the American government.

52
00:04:09.718 --> 00:04:13.279
"Conseil!" I called in an impatient voice.

53
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.465
Conseil was my servant, who had accompanied me on all my travels.

54
00:04:18.226 --> 00:04:19.886
I liked him and he liked me too.

55
00:04:20.787 --> 00:04:27.310
For the last ten years, Conseil, whose name means advice in French, had followed me wherever science had

56
00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:27.691
led me.

57
00:04:29.251 --> 00:04:32.313
Not once did he complain about the length of any journey.

58
00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:38.917
He was ready to pack his suitcase—and mine—for wherever we might be headed, whether to China or the

59
00:04:38.957 --> 00:04:45.272
Congo. Conseil was a quiet young man, never upset by any surprises that life threw our way, and

60
00:04:45.312 --> 00:04:47.294
adept at any task required of him.

61
00:04:48.374 --> 00:04:52.757
He had even become an expert in putting plants and animals into their scientific classes.

62
00:04:53.778 --> 00:04:56.480
At the age of thirty, he was ten years younger than I.

63
00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:02.043
"Conseil!" I called again as I began my feverish preparations for the journey.

64
00:05:02.944 --> 00:05:08.428
I usually didn't ask if it were convenient for him to accompany me, but this expedition could be

65
00:05:08.468 --> 00:05:10.629
hazardous. "Conseil!"

66
00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.050
I called a third time.

67
00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.651
"Did you call me?"

68
00:05:14.052 --> 00:05:15.456
he asked, entering the room.

69
00:05:15.476 --> 00:05:18.297
"Yes," I replied.

70
00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:21.638
"Pack up all our things; we're leaving in two hours.

71
00:05:21.938 --> 00:05:22.978
There's not a moment to lose."

72
00:05:22.998 --> 00:05:27.079
"And what about your collections—the fossils and animal skins?"

73
00:05:27.099 --> 00:05:30.440
"The hotel will take care of them for us," I said.

74
00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.041
"And your live wild Indonesian swine?"

75
00:05:34.061 --> 00:05:37.982
"Someone at the hotel will feed it during our absence," I answered.

76
00:05:38.002 --> 00:05:40.863
"I will give orders to forward everything to France."

77
00:05:41.924 --> 00:05:43.284
"We're not returning to Paris?"

78
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:49.986
"Eventually." I outlined our expedition on the Abraham Lincoln and asked if he wished to accompany me this

79
00:05:50.026 --> 00:05:54.527
time. "Whatever you do, Master, I'll do," Conseil replied.

80
00:05:55.427 --> 00:05:57.268
I urged him to think over his decision.

81
00:05:57.288 --> 00:06:00.069
"There's an element of danger," I said.

82
00:06:00.089 --> 00:06:04.110
"This is one of those trips that people don't always survive."

83
00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.291
"Whatever you wish," Conseil said calmly.

84
00:06:08.211 --> 00:06:11.112
He soon had our bags packed, and we settled our hotel bill.

85
00:06:12.032 --> 00:06:17.034
I left instructions for shipping my containers of stuffed animals and dried plants to Paris.

86
00:06:17.975 --> 00:06:22.577
I arranged for the purchase of supplies for the care of my swine, and with Conseil at my

87
00:06:22.637 --> 00:06:24.217
heels, I jumped into a cab.

88
00:06:25.138 --> 00:06:29.199
At the dock in Brooklyn, our luggage was immediately taken to the deck of the Abraham Lincoln.

89
00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.981
We hurried onboard and I asked for Captain Farragut.

90
00:06:33.741 --> 00:06:36.963
One of the sailors led me to a small deck on the stern of the ship.

91
00:06:37.983 --> 00:06:39.764
A handsome officer held out his hand to me.

92
00:06:40.576 --> 00:06:42.237
"Professor Pierre Aronnax?"

93
00:06:42.657 --> 00:06:42.937
he said.

94
00:06:42.957 --> 00:06:47.138
"Yes, and you must be Captain Farragut."

95
00:06:48.038 --> 00:06:48.858
The officer nodded.

96
00:06:48.878 --> 00:06:50.419
"Welcome, Professor.

97
00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:52.179
Your cabin is ready for you."

98
00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:57.621
I let Conseil stow away our luggage while I watched Captain Farragut give the orders for the ship

99
00:06:57.641 --> 00:06:58.321
to cast off.

100
00:06:59.301 --> 00:07:01.202
A few minutes later and we would have missed the boat!

101
00:07:01.902 --> 00:07:04.883
The Abraham Lincoln moved majestically away from the pier.

102
00:07:05.843 --> 00:07:09.324
The docks on both sides of the East River were crowded with spectators.

103
00:07:10.248 --> 00:07:15.550
Half a million people cheered and waved their handkerchiefs as the warship moved down the river, toward the

104
00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:21.612
harbor. By nightfall we had left the lights of the city behind and were running at full steam

105
00:07:22.013 --> 00:07:23.673
into the dark waters of the Atlantic.
